#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# domain

#% text
# `domain(f)` gives the interval `[lo, hi]` on which the interpolating function `f`, a numerical solution of a differential equation, has values.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `f` may be an `interpolating_function` from `n_dsolve` or `parametric_n_dsolve_value`, or the function of `t` that `n_dsolve_value` gives.

#% item
# A stopping event ends the solution early; the domain then ends at the event.

#% item
# Outside its domain, a call `f(t)` gives `failure(out_of_range, t, [lo, hi])`.

#% item
# For any other argument, `domain(f)` stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The domain of a solution on [0, 2]:

#% cell
domain(n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == -y(t), y(0) == 1], y(t), [t, 0, 2]))
#% output
#= [0.0, 2.0]

#% text
# A stopping event ends the solution of x'' = -x, x(0) = 1, where x first reaches zero, at pi/2:

#% cell
f = parametric_n_dsolve_value([x''(z) == -x(z), x(0) == xc, x'(0) == 0], x, [z, 0, 10], [xc], events: [x(z) == 0]);
domain(f(1.0))
#% output
#= [0.0, 1.5707963237654676]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_dsolve`, `n_dsolve_value`, `parametric_n_dsolve_value`, `interpolating_function`
